    5. OTHER NONFUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS


5.1 Performance Requirements

The Cybersecurity Awareness Platform should provide acceptable performance while users access different features of the system.

The system should respond efficiently to user requests such as login, training content access, phishing simulations, quiz submission, and performance tracking.

The following performance requirements are identified:

NFR-1: The system shall provide a reasonable response time for user requests under normal operating conditions.

NFR-2: The system shall process user login requests and display the appropriate result without unnecessary delay.

NFR-3: The system shall retrieve and display cybersecurity training content efficiently.

NFR-4: The system shall load phishing simulation scenarios within an acceptable time.

NFR-5: The system shall process quiz submissions and calculate quiz results correctly.

NFR-6: The system shall retrieve and display user performance and progress information efficiently.

NFR-7: The system should support multiple users accessing the system under normal expected usage conditions.

NFR-8: The system should maintain acceptable performance as the amount of training content, quiz data, and user information increases.


5.2 Safety Requirements

The Cybersecurity Awareness Platform is an educational software system and does not directly control physical hardware or perform operations that may cause physical harm.

However, the system must ensure that cybersecurity training and phishing simulations are safe for users.

The following safety requirements are identified:

NFR-9: The system shall provide phishing simulations only for educational and awareness purposes.

NFR-10: The system shall not perform actual phishing attacks.

NFR-11: The system shall not send malicious emails or harmful links to users.

NFR-12: The system shall not distribute malware, viruses, or other malicious software.

NFR-13: The system shall clearly distinguish simulated phishing scenarios from real cybersecurity threats.

NFR-14: The system should provide appropriate information to prevent users from misunderstanding simulation activities.

NFR-15: The system shall ensure that educational content does not intentionally expose users to harmful or unsafe activities.


5.3 Security Requirements

Security is an important requirement of the Cybersecurity Awareness Platform because the system stores user information, authentication-related data, performance records, and other application data.

The system should implement appropriate security practices to protect user information and prevent unauthorized access.

The following security requirements are identified:

NFR-16: The system shall require user authentication before allowing access to protected features.

NFR-17: The system shall validate user credentials before granting access.

NFR-18: The system shall restrict unauthorized users from accessing protected resources.

NFR-19: The system shall implement appropriate access control for administrator functions.

NFR-20: The system shall validate user input before processing it.

NFR-21: The system should store user passwords securely and should not store passwords in plain text.

NFR-22: The system should protect sensitive user information from unauthorized access.

NFR-23: The system should use secure communication practices when deployed in a production environment.

NFR-24: The system should use HTTPS for secure communication between the client and server in a production environment.

NFR-25: The system shall provide appropriate error messages without exposing sensitive system information.

NFR-26: The system should protect the application against common unauthorized access attempts.


5.4 Software Quality Attributes


5.4.1 Usability

The system should be easy to understand and use.

The user interface should be designed for users with different levels of cybersecurity and technical knowledge.

NFR-27: The system shall provide a simple and user-friendly interface.

NFR-28: The system shall provide clear navigation between different features.

NFR-29: The system shall display information in a clear and understandable manner.

NFR-30: The system should provide clear instructions for using training, simulation, and quiz features.

NFR-31: The system should display appropriate feedback and error messages.


5.4.2 Reliability

The system should operate reliably under normal conditions.

NFR-32: The system shall perform the specified functions correctly.

NFR-33: The system shall calculate quiz results accurately.

NFR-34: The system shall store and retrieve application data correctly.

NFR-35: The system should maintain data consistency during normal operation.

NFR-36: The system should handle invalid user input appropriately.


5.4.3 Maintainability

The system should be easy to maintain and modify.

A modular system structure will make it easier to update or improve individual features.

NFR-37: The system should be designed using a modular architecture.

NFR-38: The system should allow individual modules to be updated without significantly affecting unrelated modules.

NFR-39: The source code should be properly organized and documented.

NFR-40: The project should use version control for source code management.


5.4.4 Scalability

The system should support future expansion.

New cybersecurity topics, training materials, simulation scenarios, and quizzes may be added in the future.

NFR-41: The system should support the addition of new training content.

NFR-42: The system should support the addition of new phishing simulation scenarios.

NFR-43: The system should support the addition of new quiz questions and quizzes.

NFR-44: The system architecture should support future feature enhancements.


5.4.5 Availability

The system should be available to users when the application is operational.

NFR-45: The system should be accessible through a supported web browser.

NFR-46: The system should be available whenever the server and required services are operational.

NFR-47: The system should handle temporary service failures appropriately.


5.5 Business Rules

The following business rules apply to the Cybersecurity Awareness Platform:

BR-1: Only registered users shall be allowed to access personalized features of the system.

BR-2: Users must provide valid credentials to access protected areas of the application.

BR-3: Only authorized administrators shall be allowed to access administrative functions.

BR-4: Phishing simulations shall be used only for cybersecurity education and awareness.

BR-5: The system shall not perform real phishing attacks.

BR-6: The system shall not distribute malicious content, malware, or harmful links.

BR-7: Quiz results shall be calculated based on the user's submitted answers.

BR-8: User performance information shall be associated with the appropriate user account.

BR-9: Training content and quiz questions shall be managed by authorized administrators.

BR-10: Users should follow the intended educational purpose of the platform.








