6. OTHER REQUIREMENTS


6.1 Data Requirements

The Cybersecurity Awareness Platform requires the storage and management of different types of application data.

The major data requirements include:

• User registration information.

• User authentication information.

• User profile information.

• Cybersecurity training content.

• Phishing simulation scenarios.

• Quiz questions and answers.

• User quiz results.

• User simulation results.

• User learning progress.

• Administrative content information.

The system should store the required data in an organized manner and allow authorized components of the system to retrieve and update the data when required.


6.2 Backup and Recovery Requirements

The application data should be protected against accidental loss or corruption.

The system should support appropriate backup and recovery mechanisms when deployed in a production environment.

The following requirements are identified:

NFR-48: The system should support backup of important application data.

NFR-49: The system should allow recovery of important data when backup information is available.

NFR-50: The system should maintain data consistency during data storage and recovery operations.


6.3 Future Enhancement Requirements

The proposed system may be enhanced in the future based on user requirements and technological developments.

Possible future enhancements include:

• Advanced phishing simulation scenarios.

• Personalized cybersecurity learning recommendations.

• Detailed performance analytics.

• Graphical progress reports.

• Certificate generation after successful completion of training.

• Email notifications and reminders.

• Multi-language support.

• Additional cybersecurity awareness topics.

• Integration with other educational or training platforms.

• Mobile application support.


APPENDIX A: GLOSSARY


Administrator

An authorized user who manages training content, quiz questions, and other administrative functions of the system.


Authentication

The process of verifying the identity of a user before granting access to the system.


Cybersecurity

The practice of protecting computer systems, networks, applications, and information from digital threats and unauthorized access.


Dashboard

A user interface that provides access to the main features and information of the application.


Database

A structured collection of data used by the application to store and retrieve information.


End User

A person who uses the Cybersecurity Awareness Platform for learning and improving cybersecurity awareness.


Feedback

Information provided by the system to help users understand their performance and identify areas for improvement.


Frontend

The part of the application that users interact with directly through the web interface.


Backend

The server-side part of the application responsible for processing requests, implementing business logic, and communicating with the database.


HTTPS

Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure. A secure version of HTTP used for communication between a client and a server.


Malicious Link

A link designed to redirect users to harmful, fraudulent, or unauthorized web content.


Phishing

A type of cyber attack in which attackers attempt to trick users into revealing sensitive information or performing unsafe actions.


Phishing Simulation

A safe and educational activity that simulates phishing-related scenarios to help users identify phishing attempts.


Quiz

An interactive set of questions used to evaluate a user's knowledge and understanding.


Requirement

A condition, capability, or feature that the software system must provide.


SRS

Software Requirements Specification. A document that defines the functional and non-functional requirements of a software system.


Training Module

A module of the application that provides cybersecurity awareness and educational content.


User

A person who interacts with the Cybersecurity Awareness Platform.


User Interface

The part of the system through which users interact with the application.


APPENDIX B: ANALYSIS MODELS


B.1 Use Case Model

The Cybersecurity Awareness Platform involves the following primary actors:

1. End User

The End User can perform the following activities:

• Register an account.

• Log in to the system.

• Access cybersecurity awareness training.

• View phishing-related learning content.

• Participate in phishing simulations.

• Attempt cybersecurity quizzes.

• View quiz results.

• Receive feedback.

• View performance.

• Track learning progress.


2. Administrator

The Administrator can perform the following activities:

• Log in to the system.

• Manage training content.

• Add cybersecurity learning material.

• Update cybersecurity learning material.

• Manage quiz questions.

• Monitor relevant system information.


B.2 System Interaction Overview

The general interaction between the user and the system is as follows:

End User
    ↓
Registration / Login
    ↓
User Authentication
    ↓
User Dashboard
    ↓
Select Feature
    ↓
────────────────────────────────────
↓              ↓              ↓
Training    Simulation       Quiz
↓              ↓              ↓
Learning     Identify         Submit
Content      Phishing         Answers
↓              ↓              ↓
────────────────────────────────────
                ↓
        Performance Assessment
                ↓
             Feedback
                ↓
         Progress Tracking


B.3 High-Level System Model

The high-level architecture of the proposed system consists of the following components:

User
   ↓
Web Browser
   ↓
Frontend Application
   ↓
Backend Server
   ↓
Database


The frontend application provides the user interface.

The backend server processes requests and implements application logic.

The database stores user information, training content, quiz questions, simulation data, performance records, and progress information.


